/**
 * The game port did not open within `GAME_PORT_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS`. Decide whether
 * that means BROKEN or merely SLOW, by asking the guest whether it is still
 * doing work, and keep waiting for as long as it is.
 *
 * Why this exists: the 300s check cannot distinguish "Terraria is sitting on an
 * interactive world-selection prompt and will never bind a port" from "ARK is
 * 68% through a 22.9 GB SteamCMD download". Both look identical from outside —
 * SSH up, game port refused. Before this, both failed the order; measured on
 * 2026-09-06, every large Steam-based family was in the second group, so
 * healthy servers were being declared failed. Simply raising the timeout would
 * fix those at the cost of making genuinely dead servers waste 45 minutes of a
 * customer's time before admitting it.
 *
 * The progress signal is deliberately family-agnostic — no per-image knowledge
 * to drift out of date:
 *   - the guest's root filesystem is growing (a download/extract is running), OR
 *   - the game container has written to its log in the last sample window (world
 *     generation, engine startup — work that need not grow the disk much).
 * Either counts. `GAME_INSTALL_STALL_LIMIT` consecutive samples with neither is
 * what "stopped" means, so a brief pause between download and world-gen phases
 * does not end it prematurely.
